<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.6.3: Using Quadratic Functions to Describe Height</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01834</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.6.3: Using Quadratic Functions to Describe Height</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e2fe3d78-f545-4d09-bcae-43cd525485c7</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The function defined by \(d=50+312t-16t^2\) gives the height in feet of a cannonball \(t\) seconds after the ball leaves the cannon.</item>
</list>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What does the term 50 tell us about the cannonball?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The 50 tells us the cannonball is fired from 50 feet off of the ground.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What does the term \(312t\) tell us about the cannonball?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The \(312t\) tells us that the initial upward velocity is 312 feet per second.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> What does \(-16t^2\) tell us about the cannonball?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The \(-16t^2\) represents the effect of gravity pulling the cannonball back to the ground.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Graph the function. Adjust the graphing window to the following boundaries: \(0&lt;x&lt;25\) and \(0&lt;y&lt;2000\).</para>
    <para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work. </para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
  <para><figure><media alt="Graph with time in seconds on \(x\)-axis and distance above ground in feet on \(y\)-axis."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/c684d9d0de5a353a7cbf137d277723b7949ac450"/></media></figure></para>
</solution></exercise>
</example>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Observe the graph and:</item>
</list>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Describe the shape of the graph. What does it tell us about the movement of the cannonball? </para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The distance from the ground increases, reaches a peak, and then decreases, which makes sense in the situation. It also looks like the distance from the ground decreases in the same way that it increased on the way up. The graph tells us the cannonball went up in the air, and then fell back down to the ground.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<para>The shape of the quadratic function is called a <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">parabola</term>. It has a “U” shape.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Estimate the maximum height the ball reaches. When does this happen?</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The cannonball gets up to a little over 1500 feet, maybe to 1600 feet, at around 10 seconds.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>The peak of the parabola, where the maximum occurs, is also called the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">vertex</term> of the graph.</para>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> Estimate when the ball hits the ground.</para>
      </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The cannonball hits the ground a little bit before 20 seconds.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<para>The <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">zero</term> of a function is where the function equals 0, or where it crosses the \(x\)-axis. In this case, there is only one zero where the cannonball hits the ground. The parabola does cross the \(x\)-axis in the negative \(x\)-values, but that does not make sense in this problem. </para>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What domain is appropriate for this function? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>The equation is a good model for the height of the cannonball from the time it is launched, \(t=0\), until the time it lands, just before \(t=20\). For times outside of these values, the function values do not mean anything in the context (for example, for \(t=21\) the function would place the cannonball underground).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


